WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:10.620
Today I am going to try to tell you the glorious
tale of AI-based human face generation and

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:15.380
showcase an absolutely unbelievable new paper
in this area.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.440
Early in this series, we covered a stunning
paper that showcased a system that could not

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:26.470
only classify an image, but write a proper
sentence on what is going on, and could cover

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.029
even highly non-trivial cases.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.080
You may be surprised, but this thing is not
recent at all.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.160
This is 4 year old news!

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.160
Insanity.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.100
Later, researchers turned this whole problem
around, and performed something that was previously

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.079
thought to be impossible.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:49.269
They started using these networks to generate
photorealistic images from a written text

00:00:49.269 --> 00:00:50.320
description.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.769
We could create new bird species by specifying
that it should have orange legs and a short

00:00:55.769 --> 00:00:56.879
yellow bill.

00:00:56.879 --> 00:01:02.589
Then, researchers at NVIDIA recognized and
addressed two shortcomings: one was that the

00:01:02.589 --> 00:01:07.979
images were not that detailed, and two, even
though we could input text, we couldn’t

00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:11.560
exert too much artistic control over the results.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.950
In came StyleGAN to the rescue, which was
then able to perform both of these difficult

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.610
tasks really well.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:23.759
Furthermore, there are some features that
are highly localized as we exert control over

00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:29.110
these images, you can see how this part of
the teeth and eyes were pinned to a particular

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:34.960
location and the algorithm just refuses to
let it go, sometimes to the detriment of its

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.409
surroundings.

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:42.330
A followup work titled StyleGAN2 addresses
all of these problems in one go.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:49.119
So, StyleGAN2 was able to perform near-perfect
synthesis of human faces, and remember, none

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.740
of these people that you see here really exist.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.740
Quite remarkable.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.240
So, how can we improve this magnificent technique?

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.490
Well, this new work can do so many things,
I don’t even know where to start.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.720
First, and most important, we now have much
much more intuitive artistic control over

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.000
the output images.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:16.290
We can add or remove a beard, make the subject
younger or older, change their hairstyle,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:22.970
make their hairline recede, put a smile on
their face, or even make their nose pointier.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:24.459
Absolute witchcraft.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.849
So why can we do all this with this new method?

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:34.170
The key idea is that it is not using a Generative
Adversarial Network, a GAN, in short.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.819
A GAN means two competing neural networks,
where one is trained to generate new images,

00:02:39.819 --> 00:02:45.250
and the other one is used to tell whether
the generated images are real or fake.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:50.450
GANs dominated this field for a long while
because of their powerful generation capabilities,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:57.030
but, on the other hand, they are quite difficult
to train and we have only limited control

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.560
over its output.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.659
Among other changes, this work disassembles
this generator network into F and G, and the

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:11.519
discriminator network into E and D, or in
other words, adds an encoder and decoder network

00:03:11.519 --> 00:03:12.739
here.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:13.959
Why?

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:19.319
The key idea is that the encoder compresses
the image data down into a representation

00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:22.000
that we can edit more easily.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.209
This is the land of beards and smiles, or
in other words, all of these intuitive features

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:32.530
that we can edit exist here, and when we are
done, we can decompress the output with the

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:36.430
decoder network and produce these beautiful
images.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:41.409
This is already incredible, but what else
can we do with this new architecture?

00:03:41.409 --> 00:03:43.470
A lot more.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:49.010
For instance, two, if we add a source and
destination subjects, their coarse, middle,

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:52.250
or fine styles can also be mixed.

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:53.849
What does that mean exactly?

00:03:53.849 --> 00:03:59.970
The coarse part means that high-level attributes,
like pose, hairstyle and face shape will resemble

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:05.900
the source subject, in other words, the child
will remain a child and inherit some of the

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.420
properties of the destination subjects.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:14.250
However, as we transition to the “fine from
source” part, the effect of the destination

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:19.980
subject will be stronger, and the source will
only be used to change the color scheme and

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.450
microstructure of this image.

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:26.310
Interestingly, it also changes the background
of the subject.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:30.710
Three, it can also perform image interpolation.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:36.500
This means that we have these four images
as starting points, and it can compute intermediate

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.990
images between them.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:44.530
You see here that as we slowly become Bill
Gates, somewhere along the way, glasses appear.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:49.140
Now note that interpolating between images
is not difficult in the slightest and has

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.560
been possible for a long-long time — all
we need to do is just compute average results

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.240
between these images.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.590
So what makes a good interpolation process?

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:04.710
Well, we are talking about good interpolation,
when each of the intermediate images make

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.420
sense and can stand on their own.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.760
I think this technique does amazingly well
at that.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:15.670
I’ll stop the process at different places,
you can see for yourself and let me know in

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.800
the comments if you agree or not.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:23.280
I also kindly thank the authors for creating
more footage just for us to showcase in this

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.280
series.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.930
That is a huge honor, thank you so much!

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:34.730
And note that StyleGAN2 appeared around December
of 2019, and now, this paper by the name “Adversarial

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:39.670
Latent Autoencoders” appeared only four
months later.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.410
Four months later.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:42.590
My goodness!

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:47.860
This is so much progress in so little time
it truly makes my head spin.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:06:04.620
What a time

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:30.030
to

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:43.970
be alive!

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.229
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

